    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so im studying for my materials class and im a bit confused. why do we actually need to put steel rebar inside concrete for foundations? i get that concrete is strong, but whats the deal with the relationship between its compressive strength and the tensile strength of the steel? like why do they need each other?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Great question — it comes down to the fact that concrete and steel have complementary weaknesses. Concrete is excellent in compression but very weak in tension (it cracks easily when pulled or bent), while steel is strong in tension, so the steel rebar carries the tensile loads that concrete simply can't handle on its own.\n\nThe reason foundations need this becomes clear when you think about how loads actually distribute: a footing under a column bends slightly as it transfers load to the soil, which puts its *bottom* surface in tension even though the overall loading is compressive. Without rebar, those tensile zones would crack and the foundation could fail, so the two materials genuinely need each other — concrete handles the compression, protects the steel from corrosion and fire, and provides mass, while steel provides the tensile strength concrete lacks.",
      "source": "target",
