    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok that makes sense. so it's basically like a partnership. but if the concrete is just sitting there solid, does the rebar actually interact with the concrete itself or is it just sitting in a hole? how does the load actually get from the concrete into the steel? like, what's physically stopping the steel from just sliding around inside the block when things get heavy?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Excellent follow-up — that's exactly the right question to ask. The answer is **bond stress**, the physical mechanism that transfers load between concrete and steel.\n\nWhen concrete cures around rebar, it grips the bar three ways: chemical adhesion at the concrete-steel interface, friction from shrinkage (concrete actually shrinks as it cures, clamping down on the bar like a vice grip), and most importantly, the **ribs/deformations** on the rebar surface. Those ribs act like tiny anchors — when the bar gets pulled, the concrete in front of each rib pushes back against it, so the bar literally cannot slide.\n\nIf the bar were smooth and perfectly lubricated, you'd be right that it could pull out under load. In fact, smooth \"plain bars\" were used in early construction and they *did* slip — which is why modern rebar is always ribbed, and why engineers specify minimum embedment lengths (called development length) so the bond along the bar's length is strong enough to develop the bar's full tensile strength before it can pull out.",
      "source": "target",
